    "id": "how-to-set-an-expiry-date-on-a-dropbox-link",
    "uuid": "how-to-set-an-expiry-date-on-a-dropbox-link",
    "slug": "how-to-set-an-expiry-date-on-a-dropbox-link",
    "title": "How to Set an Expiry Date on a Dropbox Link (Without Upgrading Dropbox)",
    "html": "<article><p>You've shared a Dropbox link with a client, a student, or your YouTube audience. The problem: that link works forever. Anyone who finds it — a month from now, a year from now — can still download your file. If you're sharing a Lightroom preset pack, a drum kit, or a course worksheet, you probably want some control over that.</p><h2 id=\"does-dropbox-support-link-expiry\">Does Dropbox have a link expiry feature?</h2><p>Yes. Dropbox lets you set an expiration date on shared links. But it's locked behind paid plans. On dropbox.com, you need at minimum Dropbox Essentials ($11.99/month) or Professional ($19.99/month) to access it. On a free Dropbox account, the option simply isn't there.</p><p>If you don't need 2TB of extra cloud storage — you just want your links to stop working after a specific date — upgrading Dropbox is a poor trade.</p><h2 id=\"how-to-set-expiry-natively\">How to set a link expiry on Dropbox (if you have a paid plan)</h2><p>Already on a paid Dropbox plan? Here are the steps:</p><ol><li>Go to dropbox.com and sign in</li><li>Hover over the file or folder you've shared</li><li>Click <strong>Share</strong>, then click the settings gear icon</li><li>Select the shared link you want to edit</li><li>Toggle <strong>Expiration</strong> on</li><li>Pick a date — the link expires at 11:59 PM in your time zone on that day</li><li>Click <strong>Save</strong></li></ol><p>The link deactivates automatically. Anyone who visits it after the expiry date gets a &quot;link not found&quot; error.</p><h2 id=\"the-problem\">The problem: you need link expiry, not a full plan upgrade</h2><p>Most creators sharing files via Dropbox are on a free or basic account. They use Dropbox for storage. What they actually need is a way to control who can access a link and for how long — not more cloud storage.</p><p>That's a different problem. And it has a cheaper solution.</p><h2 id=\"passdropit-alternative\">How to add an expiry date to any Dropbox link with Passdropit</h2><p><a href=\"https://www.passdropit.com\">Passdropit</a> wraps your existing Dropbox link in a protected gateway. Instead of sharing the raw Dropbox URL, you share a <code>passdropit.com/yourslug</code> link. That link can require a password to unlock, expire on a date you set, and stop working after a download count you choose.</p><p>Here's how to set it up:</p><ol><li>Create a free account at <a href=\"https://www.passdropit.com/register\">passdropit.com</a></li><li>Click <strong>Create New Link</strong> and connect your Dropbox file</li><li>Under expiry settings, pick a date — or set a maximum download count</li><li>Copy your Passdropit link and share it as you normally would</li></ol><p>When the date arrives, the link stops working automatically. No manual cleanup, no remembering to delete it.</p><h2 id=\"expiry-by-date-vs-downloads\">Expiry by date vs. expiry by downloads</h2><p>Dropbox's native expiry feature only supports calendar dates. Passdropit gives you two options:</p><ul><li><strong>Date expiry:</strong> &quot;This download is available until November 30.&quot; Good for limited-time offers, event materials, or course access windows.</li><li><strong>Download limit:</strong> &quot;This preset pack is available to the first 100 people.&quot; Good for exclusive early-access drops or gated content.</li></ul><p>For example: if you're a music producer dropping a free sample pack to your Discord, you can set a 500-download limit. Once 500 people grab it, the link closes. You don't have to monitor anything.</p><h2 id=\"add-a-password-too\">Add a password while you're at it</h2><p>Since you're routing the link through Passdropit, you can add a password at the same time. Share the password only with your intended audience — say, in a YouTube members post or email newsletter. Anyone who stumbles across the link but doesn't have the password can't get in.</p><p>This solves a second problem that Dropbox doesn't address at all: your link getting reshared beyond your intended audience. A subscriber shares your &quot;members only&quot; preset download in a public Reddit thread. With a raw Dropbox link, there's nothing you can do. With a Passdropit link, only people with the password can access it — and you can revoke the link any time.</p><p>For more on tracking who actually opens your links, see our guide on <a href=\"/blog/get-notified-when-your-link-is-accessed\">getting notified when your Dropbox link is accessed</a>.</p><h2 id=\"pricing-comparison\">What does it cost?</h2><p>Link expiry on Passdropit requires a Pro account ($5/month). That's the same plan that also gives you download count limits, location-based analytics, and email notifications on each access.</p><p>Here's how that stacks up against Dropbox:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Dropbox Free</th><th>Dropbox Paid</th><th>Passdropit Pro</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Link expiry by date</td><td>✗</td><td>✓ ($12–20/mo)</td><td>✓ ($5/mo)</td></tr><tr><td>Download count limit</td><td>✗</td><td>✗</td><td>✓</td></tr><tr><td>Password protection</td><td>✗</td><td>✗</td><td>✓</td></tr><tr><td>Download tracking</td><td>✗</td><td>Basic</td><td>Full (IP, location)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>If you're already using Dropbox for storage and just want link control, Passdropit Pro gives you more than Dropbox's paid tiers do — at less than a third of the price.</p><h2 id=\"practical-example\">A practical example</h2><p>Say you're releasing a free LUT pack to celebrate hitting 10K subscribers. You want it available for two weeks, then you're putting it behind your Patreon.</p><p>With a raw Dropbox link: the link stays live indefinitely. You'd have to manually delete or disable it two weeks from now — and remember to do it.</p><p>With Passdropit Pro:</p><ul><li>Set expiry to 14 days from today</li><li>Add a password you announce at the end of the video</li><li>See how many downloads you got and where from</li><li>The link turns off automatically</li></ul><p>No admin work. The link just works until it doesn't.</p><p>If you want to protect your Dropbox links without dealing with any of this, start with our guide on <a href=\"/blog/how-to-share-and-protect-a-dropbox-link\">how to share and protect a Dropbox link</a>.</p><h2 id=\"get-started\">Get started</h2><p>Create your first link free at <a href=\"https://www.passdropit.com\">passdropit.com</a>. Link expiry, password protection, and download limits are available on Pro for $5/month.</p></article>",
